<h2>06What actually drives the price</h2>
<p>Lacquered glass is quoted per square foot or square metre, and the spread between a "cheap" and "premium" quote for the exact same size usually comes down to five things:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Driver</th><th>Why it moves the price</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Glass thickness</td><td>4mm is standard for vertical cladding; 6–8mm is common for surfaces that get touched or leaned on, like counters — thicker costs more and adds weight.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Lacquer quality &amp; coats</td><td>A single thin coat is cheaper but more prone to patchiness and premature fading; multi-coat, oven-cured polyurethane lacquer costs more but holds colour and adhesion longer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Colour &amp; custom matching</td><td>Stock shades (white, black, grey) are cheapest. Custom RAL/Pantone matches, metallics, and high-saturation reds or yellows typically cost more.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Panel size &amp; cut-outs</td><td>Larger single panels reduce joints but raise breakage risk in transit; every socket, hob, or tap cut-out adds fabrication time.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Installation &amp; substrate prep</td><td>A flat, properly primed backing wall is essential — uneven substrates need levelling before the panel goes up, which is a separate line item most quotes don't show upfront.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Ask before you sign</span>Get the lacquer brand/type, coat count, and tempering confirmed <em>in writing</em> on the quote — "painted glass" alone tells you almost nothing about durability.</div>
<h2>07Maintenance and everyday care</h2>
<p>This is genuinely one of the lower-maintenance wall and splashback finishes available, provided you avoid a short list of mistakes:</p>
<h3>Do</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Wipe with a soft microfibre cloth and a standard ammonia-free glass cleaner or a mild soap-and-water solution.</li>
  <li>Dry with a clean cloth after cleaning splashbacks to avoid water-spotting on darker, high-gloss colours.</li>
  <li>Wipe up oil splatters near hobs reasonably promptly — they don't damage the glass, but they're easier to remove before they bake on.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Don't</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Don't use abrasive scouring pads, steel wool, or powder cleansers — they won't scratch the glass face but can dull the gloss over years of use.</li>
  <li>Don't use strong ammonia-based or acidic cleaners on the edges, where sealant or adhesive is exposed.</li>
  <li>Don't let suction-cup hooks, magnetic racks, or adhesive strips sit for months without checking — some residues are harder to remove from a glossy lacquer face than from matte tile.</li>
</ul>
<h2>08Durability and where it actually fails</h2>
<p>In our experience reviewing installations and talking to fabricators, lacquered glass rarely fails from the face simply wearing out. When it does fail early, it's almost always one of three things:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Edge impact</strong> — a dropped pan, a moved appliance, or a hard knock on an unprotected corner is the most common cause of cracked panels, especially near hob edges.</li>
  <li><strong>Substrate movement</strong> — if the backing wall or MDF carcass isn't fully flat and stable, stress concentrates on the glass over time and can cause hairline cracks, particularly near fixing points.</li>
  <li><strong>Poor lacquer cure</strong> — panels from fabricators that skip the heat-curing step or use a thin single coat are the ones we see yellowing, fading near windows, or showing adhesion failure (the lacquer separating slightly from the glass) within a few years.</li>
</ul>
<p>Properly fabricated and installed, expect a realistic 15–25 year service life on the glass and lacquer itself — the limiting factor is usually a kitchen renovation cycle, not the material wearing out.</p>
<h2>09Buying checklist — what to confirm before you order</h2>
<p>Use this as a literal checklist with your fabricator or contractor:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Confirmed as tempered (toughened) glass, with thickness specified in writing.</li>
  <li>Exact final dimensions and every cut-out (sockets, switches, hob, taps) measured and marked before the order is placed — not "to be adjusted later."</li>
  <li>Lacquer type and coat count disclosed (ask specifically; "premium paint" is not an answer).</li>
  <li>A physical sample in the actual chosen colour, viewed in your own room's lighting, not just a catalogue swatch.</li>
  <li>Substrate/wall flatness checked and corrected before installation begins.</li>
  <li>Edge finishing specified (polished, flat, or pencil edge) for any exposed edge.</li>
  <li>Written warranty terms covering lacquer adhesion and colour stability, not just breakage.</li>
  <li>A clear answer on lead time, since custom colours and sizes are rarely stock items.</li>
</ul>
